WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.480
A loop can look like a wall of code, but the computer is only ever following one instruction at a time. To understand it, keep your attention on the current values, and trace the arrows.

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:23.760
In a for loop, the loop variable receives one value on each pass. An if statement inside the loop asks its question again for every new value. Here, bay two is closed, so that pass follows a different path.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:36.030
A nested loop is like an odometer with two wheels. The outer loop chooses a floor. While that floor stays fixed, the inner loop visits every bay. Only after the inner loop finishes does the floor change.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:48.680
Read nested-loop code from the outside in. Floor becomes one. Bay then begins at one. At each bay, test the conditions from top to bottom. When the pass ends, return to the inner for line for the next bay.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:01:00.750
On floor one, bay one reaches the display instruction. Bay two makes the first condition true. Continue skips everything below it for this pass, then bay becomes three. Bays three and four are displayed.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:15.260
When floor becomes two, the bay loop starts again at one. Bay one is displayed, and bay two is skipped. At bay three, both parts of the truck condition are true. The message is displayed, then break leaves the bay loop. Bay four is never reached.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:28.790
Continue and break do different jobs. Continue skips the rest of only the current pass, so the same loop tries its next value. Break ends the nearest loop completely. Here it ends the bay loop, not the outer floor loop.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:42.860
On paper, make one row for every pair that is actually attempted. Write the floor and bay first, evaluate the conditions, then record the action. Do not write the pair two comma four, because break means that pass never begins.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:56.090
Remember the rhythm. Fix one outer value. Run the inner values in order. Test each if from top to bottom. Continue means next inner value. Break means leave the inner loop. A trace table makes the flow visible.
